# Byte-exactness across platforms.
#
# Fixtures here are pinned by hash. `aciR/inst/extdata/oracle-manifest.yml`
# records an MD5 and a SHA-256 for each shipped fixture, checked on every run by
# `tests/testthat/test-oracle-manifest.R`; `tests/testthat/fixtures/` holds a
# captured path pinned the same way; and `tools/oracle/` holds the harnesses
# those fixtures came from.
#
# Git's end-of-line translation defeats all of it. On a Windows checkout the
# default conversion rewrites LF to CRLF, which changes every fixture's bytes
# and so its hash, while leaving the numbers parsed out of them identical. The
# oracle grade still passes and only the identity check fails, which is the
# right outcome from the gate and the wrong state for the repository: a
# hash-pinned fixture must be byte-identical everywhere, or the hash records a
# property of the checkout rather than of the fixture.
#
# `-text` disables all conversion, so these files are delivered verbatim on
# every platform.
#
# The rules are deliberately extension-based rather than directory-based. The
# previous version named `oracle/*.csv` and `oracle/*.m`, which stopped matching
# anything the moment those files moved to `tools/oracle/` during the
# restructure for publication, silently and with no error anywhere. That is the
# second time end-of-line conversion has broken this repository's hash gates:
# once on 2026-07-15 when the rules were absent, and once on 2026-08-17 when
# they were present but pointed at a path that no longer existed. A rule keyed
# to the extension cannot be invalidated by moving a directory.

*.csv   -text
*.m     -text
